  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Hairy curtain crust (Stereum hirsutum) is a common wood-decay fungus that forms thin, shelf-like fruiting bodies on dead hardwood. It belongs to the Stereaceae family and is widespread throughout New England and other temperate regions.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Hairy upper surface",
      "description": "The top side is velvety or hairy in appearance, typically gray, brown, or reddish-brown with concentric zones",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Smooth underside",
      "description": "The pore surface underneath is smooth to slightly wrinkled and pale yellowish or white",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Thin and flexible",
      "description": "The fruiting body is papery-thin and can be peeled away from the wood substrate",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Hairy curtain crust grows on dead or dying hardwood logs, branches, and stumps in forests and wooded areas throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Unlike many shelf fungi, Stereum hirsutum is flexible enough when fresh that it can be bent without breaking, earning it the common name 'curtain crust' for its curtain-like appearance when draped on wood.",
  "confusionNotes": null,
  "contextualKnowledge": null,
